From: RNSBox <rnsbox@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] riscv: dts: sophgo: clock the SG2000 UARTs from 96 MHz and enable UART1-4

The HaLow modem talks SLIP over UART1 at 2 Mbaud. With the UART parent
left at the bootloader rate the 8250 driver cannot reach 2 Mbaud (the
ceiling is uartclk/16), and UART1 itself is disabled in the SoC dtsi, so
/dev/ttyS1 has no uartclk at all.

Pin clk_cam0_200 (the UART baud clock parent) to disppll/8 = 96 MHz:
96 MHz / 16 / 3 = 2,000,000 exactly, and the 115200 console falls on
divisor 52 (0.16% error). Enable UART1-4 without pinctrl states (the
pinctrl driver does not model these pads; S31slip muxes UART1
through /dev/mem, the other pads are board-specific) and without a reset
on uart1, matching uart0.

--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-duomodule01-evb.dts
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/sophgo/sg2000-duomodule01-evb.dts
@@ -288,7 +288,41 @@
 	};
 };
 
-/* No assigned-clock reclock: the bootloader (fip/u-boot) already leaves the
- * UART clock in a state where its 115200 console is exact, and reprogramming
- * disppll from Linux was never proven on hardware. The 8250 driver reads the
- * real rate back from the PLL/divider registers via the clocks property. */
+/*
+ * UART clock: feed the UARTs from disppll/8 = 96 MHz. 2 Mbaud is then an
+ * exact divisor (96 MHz / 16 / 3) for the HaLow/SLIP port, and the 115200
+ * console lands on divisor 52 (0.16% error). The bootloader does not leave
+ * this mux in a state Linux can rely on, so pin parent and rate here.
+ */
+&clk {
+	assigned-clocks = <&clk CLK_CAM0_200>;
+	assigned-clock-parents = <&clk CLK_DISPPLL>;
+	assigned-clock-rates = <96000000>;
+};
+
+/*
+ * UART1 is the HaLow modem / SLIP port on XGPIOA[28] TX and XGPIOA[29] RX.
+ * No pinctrl state: pinctrl does not model these pads yet, S31slip
+ * muxes them through /dev/mem. The reset controller is left in place so the
+ * driver deasserts RST_UART1 itself.
+ */
+&uart1 {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+
+/* UART2/3/4 controllers are enabled for general use. Their pads are
+ * board-specific; the per-board pin table lives in /etc/rnsbox/uart-pins.conf
+ * and the UART card in Settings shows it. UART1 is muxed by S31slip; the
+ * other pads need a board-specific fmux setup before use. */
+&uart2 {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&uart3 {
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&uart4 {
+	status = "okay";
+};
